Roll back a failed commit in UserUpdatePhraseEnd()

UserUpdatePhraseEnd() ignored the result of END. When the commit fails
with SQLITE_BUSY because another connection is reading, the transaction
stays open and keeps its lock, so the commits of all other connections and
chewing_new2() fail from then on. Roll it back instead; only the phrases
learned in that transaction are lost.

Written with assistance from Claude Code (Claude Opus 5.5).

--- a/src/userphrase-sql.c
+++ b/src/userphrase-sql.c
@@ -332,7 +332,19 @@
 
 void UserUpdatePhraseEnd(ChewingData *pgdata)
 {
-    sqlite3_exec(pgdata->static_data.db, "END", 0, 0, 0);
+    int ret;
+
+    ret = sqlite3_exec(pgdata->static_data.db, "END", 0, 0, 0);
+    if (ret != SQLITE_OK) {
+        /*
+         * The commit fails while another connection reads the database.
+         * An open transaction would keep a lock that makes the commits of
+         * every other connection and the creation of new contexts fail, so
+         * give up the phrases learned in it instead.
+         */
+        LOG_ERROR("Cannot commit userphrase updates, error = %d", ret);
+        sqlite3_exec(pgdata->static_data.db, "ROLLBACK", 0, 0, 0);
+    }
 }
 
 int UserRemovePhrase(ChewingData *pgdata, const uint16_t phoneSeq[], const char wordSeq[])
